Z||000005||fyerstarter||06-19-2001||06:33 AM||||Before having any repairs done to your car ask your mechanic to consult the labor guide and give you an estimate. There is a set number of hours for every job on every car. Most shops charge between $50-$70 an hour. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ken Wittlief||06-19-2001||10:19 AM||||Im in the process of replacing the cat on my daughters '94 saturn SL2. It cost me $120 in parts and its taking me two nights to complete - real pain to get the old nuts and bolts off without a cutting torch, and I still have to saw the old cat off.<P>  Before I started I asked the saturn dealer here how much to do the job:<BR> <BR> $725.<P>  So if I paid $120 for parts retail, they can get them for what? $60 - $90?<P>  so with all the right equip they would be charging me $650 labor? for a 1 to 2 hour job?<P>  Its getting to the point where you cant take a car into a garage to get a headlight replaced with out being handed a $300 bill.<P>  So while Im lying under my car tonight, sawing the pipe with a hacksaw, just barely able to move under there with the car on jackstands, and if I wack my hand with the hammer again like I did last night, will I complain?<P>  not while Im saving $600 for about 6 hours work (c%||||reg||1
Z||000007||Uaana||06-19-2001||10:22 AM||||I have to pick it up this morning so I'll find out what the hell they are talking about. (They were missing a banjo bolt for the calipers).<BR>Like I said before calipers were 86-106 @<BR>She got it towed. 63. I'm still not coming up where the extra 300 came from. According to her the shop guide lists the job at 2 hours 65-75 hr. add tax Sounds like we are knocking on 500. I'm almost expecting to see cross drilled rotors and titanium calipers.||||reg||8
Z||000008||Butter||06-19-2001||11:05 AM||||Hey Ken,<BR>They make a pipe cutter that raps around the pipe with a handle that can be worked back and fourth to cut pipes in tight spots.  Don't know the name of it but a buddy of mine that works at the muffer shop used one on my truck tail pipes and I thought it was a good idea to remember it.  I just hate stickin' one hand in the way of the other.<p>[This message has been edited by Butter (edited 06-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||SteveJ||06-19-2001||11:14 AM||||I was vacationing this spring in South Carolina when the pulley on the AC went bad. In danger of the serpentine belt breaking I had to have the compressor changed by the dealer. They started on it at 9am and finished by 10:30. <BR>I was charged 4 1/2 hours labor, "by the book."||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ken Wittlief||06-19-2001||11:23 AM||||thanks - I'll look into that - it must have 3 or 4 cutting wheels spaced out around it.<P> If I take the front pipe off the exhaust manifold the cat install would be a breeze, but what are the odds of getting those bolts out without breaking them? &lt;0?<P>  If I dont see that special pipe cutter I plane on getting a couple of the best blades I can find for a hacksaw - I should be able to pull the pipe down far enought to get 3 or 4 inches of movement on the saw - so it wont be that bad.<P>  the worst is behind me - the three bolts and nuts at the flange at the back end looked like they were welded together. I could not chishle the nuts off or split them with a splitter - I chishled all the way through one nut and into the bolt and the nut still wouldnt let go.<P>  took 6 heavy duty dremil cutting wheels to get the three flange bolts off.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Uaana||06-19-2001||02:00 PM||||This just in from the GF.<P>the nice machinaic man chewed me out that if we had left the calipers <BR>on, it wounldnt have been as much  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> they had to bleed and do a brake flush. <BR>so all in all not bad.  119.99 per side. 6.81 for the banjo bolt, brake <BR>fluid 4.99 labor- replace rear calipers, 138.40, brake flush 44.00 and 69.00 <BR>for R&R bleed master cylinder.  oh and time spend waiting and switching- <BR>34.50  grand total of 638.50 (towing included)<P>Oh and they advised to replace the master cylinder.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Ken Wittlief||06-19-2001||02:15 PM||||being able to lock up all four tires at 75mph: Priceless!<P> (sorry - that came out all by itself)<P> if you start a new thread with your location and ask in the subject line, maybe someone in your area knows a good place to take your car for repairs. Its really hard to find someone that wont take you to the cleaners when his boat payment is due.<P>  I've often thought, if I opened a garage doing good honest repair work, once you got a good reputation the schedule would be booked up solid - but thats not something I would want to do for a living.<P>  I wonder how much longer it will be until cars get to the point where they are almost repair free - electric motors or something - so that this whole aspect of our lives will be a thing of the past.<P>   Seriously - when is the last time anyone called a TV repair man - some things are getting extremely dependable and repair free - so much so that we throw away stuff that still works ok, just because we are sick of it and want a new one.<P>  I put a microwave oven out on the curb last night. It still nuked but the spinning plate on the bottom stopped turning - could of gotten one of those plastic wind up ones to put in it, but wife said, $88 for a new one? lets just throw it out.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Ken Wittlief||06-21-2001||10:57 AM||||just to follow up on my saturn cat replacement.<P> day Two: got the front clamp that wasnt in with the cat and some good hacksaw blades (couldnt find the pipe cutter thing) - sawed the front pipe off the cat, flung old cat out from under the car, went to put new cat on the front pipe and<P> the pipes are the same size! WT?!<P>  sat and looked at them for a while and decided maybe I need to replace the front pipe too?<P>day three: back to parts store for the 3rd time. Learned about 2 1/4" ID pipe adapters. Got another pipe clamp.<P>  ground all the rough edges off the pipes with dremil tool - hammered pipe adapter onto front of cat, slide onto front pipe - bolted rear flange together, tigheten clamps - put hangers back on - lowered car off stands - turn key<P>   wha la! New cat - $125 - three days - 6 hrs - only cut myself once.<P>  if I had let the saturn dealer do it, it would have cost me $600 more. Lets see, at my wage and tax bracket I would have to work about 12 hours to take home $600. Okay if I draw this as a distribution graph... oh never mind<P>  when I got done I realized this was the first time I had done any exhaust work on an american car - next time I'll know what Im doing.<P>  anybody need a cat put on a saturn? Ill do it for only $700 (c%||||reg||1
